<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="65574" href="schedule/2020/spring/HIST/207/65574.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2020" href="schedule/2020.xml">2020</calendarYear><term id="120201" href="schedule/2020/spring.xml">Spring 2020</term><subject id="HIST" href="schedule/2020/spring/HIST.xml">History</subject><course id="207" href="schedule/2020/spring/HIST/207.xml">Digital Documentary Publishing</course></parents><sectionNumber>A  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Description:  How were Historical documents--the letters, diaries, and other artifacts through which historians learn about the past--published in the print age? How will they be published in the digital future? 'Publishing the Past,' a part of the History Department's Source Lab initiative, introduces students to the history of documentary editing, and invites them to imagine new models for its future.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2020-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2020-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>01:00 PM</start><end>01:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MWF    </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>182</roomNumber><buildingName>Armory</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Randolph" firstName="J">Randolph, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>